    explanation: "Capacity utilization is actual output as a share of potential output. When it runs high, firms are near their physical limits: additional demand is met by bidding up inputs and wages rather than by producing more, so prices rise. That is why the Fed watches it as part of the inflation picture — it says how much slack is left in the real economy.",
